WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.260
Welcome back to our deep dive.

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:12.260
So today we're digging into a pretty massive paradox in the U.S. healthcare system.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:13.260
Yeah, it's a huge one.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:19.860
Right, because our mission today is to figure out how Puerto Rico built this, like, high-performance

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:25.140
pharmacy engine that completely beats the national average, and, you know, why the

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.860
federal government is basically forcing them to drive it on an empty tank.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.660
Well, to really understand that paradox, we have to look at how Medicare Advantage

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.380
plan funding actually works, you know, under the hood.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.100
Which comes down to the star ratings, right?

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.100
Exactly.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:44.780
Those stars dictate how much federal funding a plan gets, and they are heavily, heavily

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.980
based on patient medication adherence.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.020
Meaning are people actually taking their meds?

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.020
Right.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.060
If patients aren't consistently taking their prescriptions for chronic issues, things

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:59.340
like hypertension, diabetes, or high cholesterol, well, the plan on a star rating drops,

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:00.740
and then the funding just dries up.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:01.740
Wow, okay.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.500
To jump back to 2013, the community pharmacies in Puerto Rico, they actually saw this

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:07.500
tension early on.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.500
They did.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.500
It was really visionary.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.980
Yeah, specifically the asociación de farmacías de comunidad de Puerto Rico, or

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:14.620
AFCPR.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.740
Under the presidency of Elliot Pacheco, their board of directors realized they

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:20.140
needed a real systemic fix.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.060
And that's where the adherence program was born.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.220
I mean, the clinical protocol itself was elaborated by doctors of pharmacy

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:31.020
Nuri Echeveria and Vixima Rodriguez, along with Elto Ricardo-Cintron.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:31.500
Right.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.940
And the crazy part is they didn't just keep it to themselves.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:38.260
No, they took this unified model straight to the Medicare Advantage plans

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.220
to implement, which is a massive achievement.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.860
They identified the gap and completely shifted the focus from, you know, just

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:49.860
handing over pills to actively managing the patient's care.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.500
I actually like to think of it like a racing pit crew.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.660
Oh, well, that's a good way to put it.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:53.980
Right.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.700
Like instead of a patient making five different trips a month and

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.660
inevitably forgetting a pill, these pharmacists act like the pit crew.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.740
They do medication synchronization.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.900
They do reconciliation, making sure there are no bad drug interactions.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:06.460
Exactly.

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:10.540
They get recognized and paid for clinically tuning the engine, not just

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:12.380
handing the driver a box of spare parts.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:18.100
And the federal health data from 2020 to 2023 shows us exactly how well

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:21.100
that pit crew approach actually works in practice.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.020
The numbers are pretty wild.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:23.820
They really are.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:28.620
Island residents fill about 19% more prescriptions annually than

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:29.660
the national benchmark.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.100
Which, you know, you'd assume that kind of intensive care would

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.580
drive costs through the roof.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:35.580
But it doesn't.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:40.940
They're paying roughly 30% less per fill than the national average.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:45.700
We are talking about $63 per prescription versus $89 nationally.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.420
Wait, so they are ranked first among all 56 U.S.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.660
jurisdictions for highest utilization and lowest gross drug costs.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.020
Yes, exactly.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.500
Number one out of all 56.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:58.780
It's a staggering dual achievement.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.100
And, you know, we have to be really precise here.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.700
This isn't just patients buying meds because they have a lower price tag.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:05.500
Not at all.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:05.700
Right.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:06.540
It's highly deliberate.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:06.900
Yeah.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.380
The pharmacy profile there focuses heavily on generic treatments for

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.540
chronic conditions like thyroid and heart health.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:15.660
It is fundamentally a culture of high medical adherence.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:18.500
Which brings us right back to the paradox you mentioned at the start.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:19.620
The empty tank.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:20.300
Exactly.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.180
You had the most efficient, highly adherent jurisdiction in the country

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:26.500
hitting these five star performance levels.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.420
But because of statutory limitations,

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.860
Puerto Rico is legally boxed out of the low income subsidies

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:36.260
that residents in the other 50 states just get automatically.

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:36.980
Hold on.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:42.100
I mean, if they are ranked number one for keeping costs low and adherence high,

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:43.980
shouldn't they be drowning in federal support?

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:44.700
You would think so.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:46.980
But it's a statutory blind spot.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.740
The irony is sharp, honestly.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:53.220
The actual foregone assistance, meaning the money denied to these

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.700
low-income patients is estimated at $250 million annually.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:03:58.820
$250 million?

00:03:58.820 --> 00:03:59.260
Wow.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:03:59.540
Yeah.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:01.900
And if you look at potential parity funding,

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:06.020
treating them exactly like a state that a figure could reach up to $2 billion.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.220
So what does this mean for you listening, right?

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.220
As someone navigating the health care system,

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:13.980
it proves that this proactive community level care works.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.460
It gives us a clear blueprint.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:16.380
It does.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:20.700
And honestly, it serves as a firm recommendation to the federal government.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.940
They need to acknowledge how local providers in Puerto Rico

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:28.300
successfully work together for the benefit of the patients to reach those five stars.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:29.060
Absolutely.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:31.260
It shouldn't be some underfunded anomaly.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.380
It needs to be used as a national model.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:36.180
And it's a strong recommendation to health plans,

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:40.500
to other health care providers and to patients to continue this proactive collaboration.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.060
Yeah, you have to keep the pit crew talking to the driver.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.100
So I will leave you with this final thought to mull over.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:49.420
Consider what staggering heights of health care efficiency

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:53.460
could be reached nationwide if local on the ground innovation

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.300
was actually matched by equitable federal funding.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.060
If it runs this well on empty, imagine what it could do with a full tank.

00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:06.180
